## 🗺️ 4. Modular Frameworks (23 Frameworks across 4 Categories)

Frameworks are located in `frameworks/` and invoked via `/apply-framework <id>` or diagnosed via `/recommend-framework`:

- **Strategy**:
  - `north-star-metric-tree`: North Star Metric & Input Metric Tree — To establish a clear causal equation between day-to-day feature development and overall company success, ensuring squads optimize actionable leading indicators without gaming the system.
  - `pricing-and-packaging`: Van Westendorp & Packaging/Tiering Matrix — To establish data-backed pricing boundaries, eliminate monetization guesswork, and package features into clear, high-converting tier boundaries.
  - `product-positioning`: Product Positioning (April Dunford) — To eliminate positioning confusion, elevate differentiation above incumbent competitors, and guide GTM launch messaging, sales battlecards, and PRD scope.
  - `seven-powers-moats`: 7 Powers (Hamilton Helmer) Moat Analysis — To force product managers to distinguish between temporary "features" (easily copied commodities) and structural "powers" that generate enduring enterprise value.
  - `theory-of-constraints`: Theory of Constraints (ToC) & CRT Mapping — To pinpoint the operational bottleneck choking product delivery, maximize throughput at that specific point, and align all upstream discovery and downstream execution to eliminate waste.
  - `wardley-mapping`: Wardley Value Chain Evolution Mapping — To prevent catastrophic resource misallocation (e.g., custom-building commoditized components) and explicitly identify where a product's true competitive differentiation resides.
  - `working-backwards-prfaq`: Working Backwards (Amazon PR/FAQ) — To enforce extreme customer-centric clarity, prevent technical solipsism, align cross-functional leaders around the core value proposition, and expose unaddressed trade-offs long before development begins.
- **Discovery**:
  - `aarrr-and-growth-loops`: AARRR Pirate Metrics & Growth Loops — To diagnose exactly where the user conversion leaks occur, fix the "leaky bucket" before spending money on acquisition, and architect compounding, product-led distribution loops.
  - `assumption-mapping`: Assumption Mapping & Riskiest Assumption Test (RAT) — To eliminate leap-of-faith failures and prevent engineering teams from spending months building products or features that nobody wants, cannot be monetized, or hit insurmountable technical roadblocks.
  - `jtbd-and-mom-test`: Jobs-to-be-Done (JTBD) & The Mom Test — To uncover genuine customer demand by systematically filtering out false validation, identifying real customer struggle, and structuring value propositions around the forces that drive adoption.
  - `opportunity-solution-tree`: Opportunity Solution Tree (OST) — To break the "feature factory" anti-pattern by ensuring teams explore the problem space thoroughly, evaluate multiple competing solutions for each customer pain point, and test assumptions before committing engineering resources.
- **Prioritization**:
  - `ice-and-value-effort`: ICE & Value vs. Effort Prioritization — To deliver rapid, transparent prioritization when teams are in early stages, lack large-scale statistical telemetry for RICE, or need to unblock backlog paralysis in under 15 minutes.
  - `kano-model`: The Kano Model & Customer Delight — To construct balanced product releases that satisfy table-stakes baseline requirements, compete effectively on performance, and incorporate distinct delighters while aggressively pruning indifferent bloat.
  - `moscow-scope-negotiation`: MoSCoW & Buy-a-Feature Scope Negotiation — To establish enforceable release boundaries, protect developers from burnout, and prevent the "everything is high priority" trap during fixed-date enterprise deliveries.
  - `opportunity-scoring`: Opportunity Scoring (Importance vs. Satisfaction) — To eliminate the wasteful "feature trap" of improving capabilities that customers already find "good enough", and redirect resources toward high-importance jobs where customers remain acutely dissatisfied.
  - `rice`: RICE Scoring Matrix — To eliminate cognitive bias, loud-stakeholder lobbying, and "pet project" syndrome by anchoring prioritization to empirical reach, measurable business impact, defensible confidence, and engineering effort.
  - `wsjf-cost-of-delay`: Weighted Shortest Job First (WSJF) & Cost of Delay — To maximize economic flow by scheduling high-value, time-critical, risk-reducing features that can be delivered quickly, preventing the common failure mode of tying up engineering squads on monolithic, slow-moving projects.
- **Operations & Delivery**:
  - `gist-planning`: GIST Planning (Goals, Ideas, Step-projects, Tasks) — To drastically reduce product waste by testing ideas through tiny step-projects before committing full engineering squads to building multi-month feature implementations.
  - `kanban-continuous-flow`: Continuous Kanban & Service Level Expectations (SLE) — To eliminate sprint-end crunch and rollover anxiety, stabilize flow predictability, shorten lead time from idea to production, and ensure operational readiness when `operating_model: kanban` is active in `.context/harness-config.yaml`.
  - `pre-mortem-tigers`: Pre-Mortem (Tigers, Paper Tigers & Elephants) — To normalize the voice of dissent, prevent preventable launch disasters, clear away unproductive anxiety over minor edge cases, and confront taboo blockers while there is still time to adjust scope.
  - `scrum-sprint-delivery`: Scrum Sprint Delivery & INVEST Execution — To provide predictable, sustainable, cross-functional engineering throughput with continuous delivery of shippable software increments.
  - `shape-up`: Shape Up (Basecamp Product Development) — To eliminate endless scope creep, eliminate backlog grooming debt, and empower autonomous cross-functional teams to deliver complete, polished features within bounded timeboxes.
  - `user-story-mapping`: User Story Mapping (Jeff Patton) — To eliminate "flat backlog blindness", ensure teams ship complete, coherent end-to-end user journeys rather than disconnected vertical feature fragments, and establish clear release boundaries based on value increments.

---

## 🔄 5. Delivery Operating Models (`operating_model`)

Configured via `operating_model` in `.context/harness-config.yaml`:
- `scrum`: 1–2 week sprints, INVEST stories, Fibonacci points, DoD sign-off (Default)
- `shape-up`: 6-week cycles, shaped pitches, appetite budgeting, hill charts, circuit breaker
- `kanban`: Continuous pull flow, WIP limits, cycle-time optimization
- `gist`: Goals, Ideas (ICE scored), Step-projects (<= 2 wks), Tasks for high-uncertainty planning

---

## 🛠️ 6. Tool & Script Guidelines

- **Python Scripts**: All helper scripts in `scripts/` use Python standard library only. Do not introduce external pip dependencies.
- **Manifest**: Consult `dist/harness-manifest.json` for live machine-readable metadata on all skills, frameworks, and context files.
